```ADD_PAPER
2401.14438v2
From the Fair Distribution of Predictions to the Fair Distribution of Social Goods presents a rigorous framework that connects the fairness constraints imposed on prediction algorithms with the subsequent distribution of social goods—in this case, long-term unemployment benefits and labor market interventions. The paper methodically develops a simulation study using Swiss administrative data, where various fairness criteria (such as statistical parity and equality of opportunity) are enforced. Extensive experimental results are discussed, highlighting that imposing fairness on predictions may reduce the overall effectiveness of policy interventions. The simulations reveal that while fairness constraints improve the equity of predictions, they could inadvertently hinder targeted intervention strategies, thereby affecting gender disparities and overall unemployment rates. These detailed experimental analyses, which quantify the trade-offs between improving predictive accuracy versus expanding screening access, are directly relevant to devising protocols for assessing the prediction-access ratio (PAR). This work offers significant insights for modeling, simulation, and evaluation strategies aimed at identifying worst-off individuals in the labor market.
``` 
~~~~~~~~~~~
