```ADD_PAPER
2501.19334v3
This paper develops a novel framework where the Prediction-Access Ratio (PAR) is introduced to quantify the relative benefit of improving predictive accuracy versus expanding screening access. Focusing on German long-term unemployment, the authors combine theoretical analysis with detailed simulation models using administrative data. In their comprehensive experimental setup, various scenarios are simulated to understand the trade-offs between enhancing prediction quality and widening access to identify the worst-off. The analysis rigorously examines how changes in prediction performance, influenced by wage shocks, duration of benefit receipt, and social network effects, impact the overall welfare outcomes. Experimental results reveal distinct thresholds: beyond a certain point, increased screening access offers diminishing returns relative to improvements in prediction accuracy. This work provides a replicable protocol for model selection and validation, offering vital insights for policymakers aiming to design better-targeted labor market interventions.
``` 
~~~~~~~~~~~
