Fairness in Algorithmic Profiling: A German Case Study introduces a robust evaluation framework for profiling long-term unemployed individuals by predicting risks of enduring unemployment. The study focuses on German administrative data to simulate a variety of intervention scenarios, exploring how modifications in prediction accuracy and screening access influence eventual welfare outcomes. Notably, the paper rigorously reproduces theoretical analyses by demonstrating that beyond a certain threshold, enhancements in screening access yield diminishing benefits compared to improvements in model prediction performance. Detailed simulation experiments examine variations in model inputs—such as socio-demographic features and local labor market conditions—and assess their effects on fairness metrics and policy targeting efficiency. These experimental results validate the use of a prediction-access ratio (PAR) as a key metric, providing actionable insights for policymakers aiming to optimally allocate labor market support to the worst-off.
``` 
