Non-Take-Up of Unemployment Benefit II in Germany leverages a detailed microsimulation model to analyze how long-term behavioral dynamics affect benefit claiming among eligible households. Importantly, the paper offers a rigorous simulation protocol that dissects the balance between enhancing predictive precision and expanding screening access to identify the most vulnerable segments of the labor market. By integrating administrative datasets with the GETTSIM model, the study conducts extensive sensitivity analyses on variables such as income dynamics and past benefit receipt histories. Its experimental setup quantifies thresholds at which marginal gains in prediction accuracy become more valuable than broadening access to screening, effectively framing a prediction-access ratio (PAR) metric. These simulation outcomes not only reproduce theoretical insights but also provide a replicable framework for policymakers targeting long-term unemployment challenges.
