2211.12437v1
Macroeconomic Effects of Active Labour Market Policies: A Novel Instrumental Variables Approach develops a simulation-based framework to assess the impact of various labor market interventions on long-term unemployment in Germany. Although the primary focus is on estimating the causal effects of active labor market policies using instrumental variables and microsimulation techniques, the paper’s extensive sensitivity analyses and dynamic evaluation protocols offer a robust blueprint for assessing non-linear policy effects. These methodologies can be naturally extended to evaluate the trade-offs in prediction-based screening: specifically, quantifying the benefits of more accurate predictive models against those achieved by broadening screening access, a comparison captured by the prediction-access ratio (PAR). The detailed experimental results, including simulations under different economic scenarios, provide valuable insights into how targeted interventions might be optimized to support the worst-off, complementing and enhancing traditional policy evaluations.
``` 
