Postdoc:  ```PLAN
Title: Adaptive Bayesian Online Optimization Approach for Quantifying Prediction-Access Trade-offs in Screening

Overview:
We propose a dual-track experimental design that integrates advanced Bayesian modeling with online optimization techniques to evaluate the prediction-access ratio (PAR). Our goal is to quantify the relative value of enhancing prediction accuracy versus expanding screening access for identifying the worst-off, replicating theoretical insights and the German long-term unemployment case study.

I. Theoretical Simulation Track
1. Synthetic Data Generation:
   • Generate outcomes Y from three families:
     – Gaussian (baseline symmetric distribution)
     – Skewed Gamma (alternative to log-normal capturing asymmetry)
     – Heavy-tailed Pareto (to account for extreme risk behaviors)
   • Simulate predictor variables X and introduce controllable noise. In a Bayesian linear regression framework, adjust R² by tuning the prior variance on coefficients.

2. Bayesian Modeling:
   • Use Bayesian linear regression to predict Y from X, capturing parameter uncertainty via posterior distributions.
   • Generate posterior predictive distributions—this will help modulate effective R² levels and simulate both low and high prediction performance scenarios.

3. Screening Rule via Online Optimization:
   • Define the screening threshold using the β-quantile of Y. The objective is to maximize V(α, f; β) = Pr[screened | Y ≤ t(β)] where t(β) is the β threshold. 
   • Replace static thresholds with an online optimization approach: employ stochastic gradient descent (SGD) to iteratively adjust the threshold.
   • Optimize a custom loss function that penalizes misclassification (i.e., failing to screen truly worst-off individuals) and includes a cost component scaling by the ratio C_access/C_pred. This cost-adjusted loss provides explicit trade-offs between expanding screening (α) and enhancing prediction performance (as captured by R²).

4. Parameter Sweeps and Improvements:
   • Vary screening capacity α ∈ {0.01, 0.02, …, 0.50} and thresholds β ∈ {0.05, 0.15, 0.25}.
   • Explore both local (infinitesimal Δα, ΔR² = 0.01) and finite improvements (e.g., Δα, ΔR² ∈ {0.05, 0.10}).
   • Simulate multiple regimes: scarce access (α ≪ β), moderate prediction (R² ≈ 0.15–0.5), near-random (R² → 0), and near-perfect prediction (R² → 1).

5. Visualization & Diagnostic Outputs:
   • Generate multidimensional PAR surfaces, screening policy visualizations, and policy value curves.
   • Plot local improvement rates and include uncertainty quantification (e.g., bootstrapped confidence intervals) to assess robustness across model variations.

II. Empirical Track Using German Unemployment Data (or High-Fidelity Synthetic Proxy)
1. Data and Baseline Modeling:
   • Source German long-term unemployment administrative data or construct a high-fidelity synthetic dataset mimicking its key properties (socio-demographics, labor market histories).
   • Begin with a Bayesian logistic regression to estimate the risk of prolonged unemployment, emphasizing probabilistic outputs.

2. Advanced Predictive Modeling:
   • Transition to ensemble methods (e.g., XGBoost) to capture non-linear relationships.
   • Compare the posterior predictions from Bayesian logistic regression versus the ensemble model to study differences in PAR outcomes.

3. Adaptive Screening Implementation:
   • Apply the same online optimization technique developed in the theoretical track. Adjust the screening threshold iteratively to maximize a cost-sensitive objective function.
   • Monitor how adaptive threshold updates affect the PAR, especially when overlaid with the cost function C_access/C_pred.

4. Evaluation and Fairness Analysis:
   • Visualize empirical PAR surfaces and policy value curves; include subgroup analyses (e.g., by age, gender, and region) to identify differential impacts.
   • Perform robustness checks via bootstrapping to generate uncertainty bands around key performance metrics.

III. Documentation and Reporting:
   • Clearly state the policy objective: to maximize V(α, f; β) and to assess the marginal gains from increasing screening capacity versus improving prediction accuracy.
   • Document assumptions (monotonicity of the screening rule, stable deployment distribution, etc.) and note any deviations.
   • Replicate figures similar to those in the literature (e.g., screening policy visualization, PAR vs α curves, residual diagnostics).

Timeline & Implementation:
   • Phase 1: Develop simulation protocols in Python using libraries such as PyMC3 (or Stan) for Bayesian modeling and TensorFlow/PyTorch for online optimization.
   • Phase 2: Validate simulation results, perform systematic parameter sweeps, and generate preliminary visualizations.
   • Phase 3: Transition to the empirical track, apply models on the German unemployment data/synthetic proxy, and integrate adaptive screening.
   • Phase 4: Conduct sensitivity analyses, prepare a detailed report, and outline policy recommendations based on cost-adjusted PAR findings.

This plan unifies advanced Bayesian techniques with cost-sensitive online optimization to yield novel insights into screening policies, extending previous work with robust uncertainty quantification and adaptive decision-making strategies.

Key Innovations:
   • Bayesian framework for parameter uncertainty and R² modulation.
   • Online SGD-based threshold optimization integrating cost sensitivity.
   • Comparative evaluation across diverse outcome distributions and modeling paradigms.
   • Rigorous dual-track analysis combining simulation and empirical case study.

End of Plan.
  
