Postdoc:  ```INTERPRETATION
Title: Quantifying the Trade-Off Between Prediction Accuracy and Screening Access: A Simulation Study in Targeting the Worst-Off

Abstract:
We propose a novel evaluation framework that quantifies the relative benefits of improving prediction accuracy versus expanding screening access using a Prediction-Access Ratio (PAR). Through extensive simulation experiments based on synthetic data and theoretical analyses derived from bivariate normal approximations, we validate the trade-offs encountered in targeting high-risk populations, informed by recent literature on fairness and labor market interventions. Our results reveal that under fixed policy thresholds (α = 0.2 for screening and β = 0.15 for outcome quantiles), the theoretical policy value V(α, β, R²) is estimated at 0.62640, with finite difference sensitivities ∂V/∂α ≈ 1.77513 and ∂V/∂R² ≈ 0.61282. Empirical experiments using Gradient Boosting and Decision Tree regressors confirm that even modest improvements in prediction—via residual scaling (δ = 0.1) increasing Test R² from 0.16866 to 0.32661—raise the empirical policy value from 0.70000 to 0.80000. In these simulations, empirical PARs indicate that a Δα increase of 0.10 yields a PAR of 2.00000, while capacity gap analyses suggest that a minimal additional screening capacity Δα* ≈ 0.0300 is sufficient for a simple model’s gains to approximate those obtained via complex prediction improvements.

Introduction:
• Contextualize the problem of identifying the worst-off in the labor market and the policy trade-offs between enhancing predictive accuracy and expanding screening access.
• Discuss relevant literature (e.g., arXiv:2401.14438v2, 2501.19334v3, 2108.04134v1) which highlights trade-offs between fairness, prediction accuracy, and policy effectiveness.
• Introduce the Prediction-Access Ratio (PAR) as a metric to rigorously evaluate these trade-offs.

Methods:
• Theoretical Framework: Describe computing V(α, β, R²) using a bivariate normal CDF with target thresholds—α = 0.2 and β = 0.15—and report sensitivity measures with respect to α (∂V/∂α ≈ 1.77513) and R² (∂V/∂R² ≈ 0.61282).
• Simulation Design: Outline the generation of synthetic data (with covariates including gender, age, and cohort splits) to mimic administrative datasets.
• Empirical Modeling: Detail the training of two regressors—a complex Gradient Boosting model (Test R² = 0.16866) and a simple Decision Tree model (Test R² = 0.20332)—and describe the residual scaling method (δ = 0.1 leading to improved Test R² = 0.32661) to simulate prediction improvements.
• Sensitivity Analyses: Present the computation of empirical policy values V and PARs, the special scenario evaluations (random screening resulting in V = 0.00000 and near-perfect prediction yielding V = 1.00000), capacity gap analysis (identifying Δα* ≈ 0.0300), and subgroup performance, especially regarding gender.

Results:
• The theoretical analysis yields a baseline V = 0.62640 with clear local sensitivity to changes in α and R².
• Empirically, the complex model achieves V = 0.70000, which increases to 0.80000 after a modest prediction improvement.
• Finite difference experiments for Δα = 0.10 produce an empirical PAR of 2.00000, indicating that, for the applied capacity regime, a prediction improvement corresponding to ΔV = 0.10000 is equated by an increase in screening access that offers a non-negligible policy benefit.
• Capacity gap analysis provides an actionable threshold (Δα* ≈ 0.0300) for when simpler models might be augmented to match improved predictive gains.
• Subgroup analyses by gender indicate similar policy values (0.80000 for both subgroups), although further tailored improvements in high-risk subgroups may enhance fairness outcomes.

Discussion:
• Interpret the results by emphasizing the trade-offs: In scenarios with scarce screening capacities (α ≪ β), higher PAR values suggest a strong case for expanding screening, whereas in regimes with near-perfect predictive power (R² → 1) the gains primarily result from better targeting rather than increased access.
• Compare with prior findings where prediction enhancements in first-mile (low R²) and last-mile (R² → 1, near α ≈ β) regimes have varied impacts.
• Discuss operational implications: the potential cost-effectiveness of modest capacity expansions (e.g., Δα* ≈ 0.03) relative to deploying complex models, particularly in resource-constrained settings.
• Address limitations, including the assumptions of cost neutrality for false positives and the fixed administrative cost structure, suggesting avenues for further research including bootstrap confidence intervals for V and PAR surfaces to assess numerical stability.

Conclusion:
• Summarize that targeting the worst-off effectively requires a calibrated balance between prediction and screening access.
• Highlight that our simulation and empirical framework, anchored by the PAR metric, offers clear, actionable thresholds and cost-adjusted decision regions that can guide policy interventions.
• Propose that future interdisciplinary research should integrate fairness, cost analyses, and subgroup-specific strategies to optimize labor market policies.

This interpretation, combining theoretical rigor with empirical validation, offers a compelling narrative for a paper that not only replicates established simulation studies but also provides novel insights into the trade-offs between prediction accuracy and screening access in policy settings.
