Reviews: Reviewer #1:
(7.295454545454544, 'The performance of your submission is: 7.295454545454544THOUGHT:\nThe paper presents a comprehensive framework for quantifying trade-offs in policy evaluation. I appreciate the theoretical derivation of a policy value function and the novel Prediction-Access Ratio (PAR) metric, as well as the clear exposition of methods and simulation experiments. However, several concerns remain. The reliance solely on synthetic data raises questions about real-world applicability, and the assumptions (e.g., bivariate normality, homoscedastic errors) could be too restrictive in practice. While the residual scaling technique is interesting as an efficient approximation method, its robustness across diverse settings is not fully demonstrated. Finally, although the discussion of fairness and subgroup analyses is present, additional work on empirical validations (with real administrative datasets or varying distributions) would strengthen the contribution considerably. My overall view is positive but cautious due to these limitations.\n\nREVIEW JSON:\n```json\n{\n  "Summary": "The paper introduces a theoretical framework to quantify the trade-off between predictive accuracy and screening access in policy evaluation. It defines a policy value function based on a bivariate normal approximation and introduces the Prediction-Access Ratio (PAR) to compare improvements achieved by increasing screening thresholds versus enhancing predictive performance. The authors validate their derivations with extensive simulation experiments on synthetic data and perform capacity gap analyses, drawing actionable insights for resource allocation and policy interventions.",\n  "Strengths": [\n    "Solid theoretical derivation of a policy value function capturing nonlinear sensitivities.",\n    "Novel introduction of the Prediction-Access Ratio (PAR) as a comparative metric.",\n    "Clear and comprehensive presentation with all major sections (abstract, introduction, methods, results, discussion).",\n    "Detailed simulation experiments and sensitivity analyses that elucidate the trade-offs.",\n    "Discussion includes practical implications and future research directions."\n  ],\n  "Weaknesses": [\n    "Heavy reliance on synthetic data without validation on real-world datasets.",\n    "Assumptions such as bivariate normality and homoscedasticity may limit generalizability.",\n    "Limited exploration of the robustness of the residual scaling technique across diverse settings.",\n    "Fairness analyses are limited to gender subgroups, and further demographic breakdowns would be beneficial.",\n    "The ethical considerations and potential negative societal impacts are mentioned only superficially."\n  ],\n  "Originality": 3,\n  "Quality": 3,\n  "Clarity": 3,\n  "Significance": 3,\n  "Questions": "1. Can the authors provide any evidence or preliminary results on the performance of the framework with real-world (non-synthetic) data? 2. How sensitive are the conclusions to violations of the bivariate normality or homoscedasticity assumptions? 3. Could the authors elaborate on the limitations of the residual scaling method and its potential impact on the estimates in less ideal conditions? 4. Are there plans to extend subgroup analyses beyond gender to uncover potential disparities affecting other demographic groups?",\n  "Limitations": "The study is limited by its reliance on synthetic datasets and assumptions that may not hold in real-world applications. The work does not fully explore the implications of noisy or heavy-tailed error distributions, and the scalability and robustness of the residual scaling technique remain to be tested extensively. Additionally, while ethical considerations are briefly mentioned, a more detailed analysis of potential negative societal impacts would strengthen the work.",\n  "Ethical Concerns": false,\n  "Soundness": 3,\n  "Presentation": 3,\n  "Contribution": 3,\n  "Overall": 7,\n  "Confidence": 4,\n  "Decision": "Accept"\n}\n```', True), 
Reviewer #2:
(7.295454545454544, 'The performance of your submission is: 7.295454545454544THOUGHT:\nThis paper proposes a quantitative framework for evaluating trade‐offs in policy evaluation by jointly considering prediction accuracy (measured by R²) and screening access thresholds through a novel policy value function and the derived Prediction-Access Ratio (PAR). The paper is methodologically rigorous, presenting detailed derivations, simulation experiments with synthetic data and addressing both local sensitivities and capacity gap analyses. However, I have concerns about the heavy reliance on synthetic data and strong distributional assumptions (notably bivariate normality), which could limit the real-world applicability of the results. The clarity is mostly good, though some sections are highly technical and might benefit from further explanation, especially regarding the generalizability of the results. While the idea is original and potentially impactful in informing resource allocation in policy settings, the work would be stronger if validated on real data and if additional discussion on robustness to assumption violations were provided.\n\nREVIEW JSON:\n{\n  "Summary": "The paper introduces a comprehensive framework to quantify the trade-off between prediction accuracy and screening access in policy evaluation. Central to the work is a policy value function, V(α, β, R²), based on the bivariate normal cumulative distribution, from which the authors derive local sensitivity metrics and a Prediction-Access Ratio (PAR). The theoretical formulation is supported by extensive simulation experiments using synthetic data, comparing complex and simpler predictive models and demonstrating that modest adjustments in screening thresholds can yield gains similar to improvements in predictive accuracy.",\n  "Strengths": [\n    "Introduces a novel metric (PAR) that directly compares gains from prediction enhancements and increased screening access.",\n    "Provides rigorous mathematical derivations and sensitivity analyses.",\n    "Comprehensive experimental setup with detailed simulation results and capacity gap analysis.",\n    "Relevant discussion linking model improvements with operational policy interventions."\n  ],\n  "Weaknesses": [\n    "Heavy reliance on synthetic data without real-world validation limits the immediate applicability.",\n    "Assumptions (e.g., bivariate normality, homoscedasticity) may not hold in practice, and sensitivity to these assumptions is not adequately explored.",\n    "Some technical details and derivations could be more accessible to a broader audience.",\n    "The ethical and societal implications, while mentioned, could be discussed in greater depth."\n  ],\n  "Originality": 3,\n  "Quality": 3,\n  "Clarity": 3,\n  "Significance": 3,\n  "Questions": "1. How sensitive are the results to deviations from the bivariate normal assumption? Would similar trade-offs be observed with real-world data exhibiting heavy tails or heteroscedasticity? 2. Can the authors provide any preliminary results or case studies using actual administrative or field data to support the synthetic simulations? 3. How robust is the residual scaling approach across different modeling frameworks and parameter choices? 4. Could the authors elaborate on potential strategies for extending the framework to dynamic or longitudinal settings?",\n  "Limitations": "The paper relies exclusively on synthetic datasets and assumptions (e.g., bivariate normality), which may not capture the complexity of real-world scenarios. Additionally, while the proposed metrics are insightful, the lack of empirical validation on diverse datasets limits understanding of their practical robustness. More discussion on the potential negative societal impacts—or risks of misinterpretation when applied to sensitive policy areas—would further strengthen the work.",\n  "Ethical Concerns": false,\n  "Soundness": 3,\n  "Presentation": 3,\n  "Contribution": 3,\n  "Overall": 7,\n  "Confidence": 4,\n  "Decision": "Accept"\n}', True), 
Reviewer #3:
(7.295454545454544, 'The performance of your submission is: 7.295454545454544THOUGHT:\nThe paper presents a comprehensive framework for quantifying the trade-offs between prediction accuracy and screening access in policy evaluation. I appreciate the mathematical derivation of the policy value function V(α,β,R²) and the introduction of the Prediction-Access Ratio (PAR), which together offer actionable insights on whether to invest in more complex model improvements or in increasing screening capacity. The simulation experiments with synthetic data provide a consistent demonstration of the theoretical claims. However, the work relies heavily on the bivariate normal assumption and synthetic data, which may limit its applicability to real-world settings. I would have liked to see a discussion on how robust the findings are under distributional shifts or non-Gaussian errors, as well as an evaluation on real administrative data. Overall, while the idea is interesting and the technical presentation is rigorous, these limitations and reliance on approximate methods could reduce its impact in practice.\n\nREVIEW JSON:\n{\n  "Summary": "The paper introduces a rigorous framework to quantify the trade-off between prediction accuracy and screening access in policy evaluation. It defines a policy value function V(α,β,R²) based on the bivariate normal distribution and introduces the Prediction-Access Ratio (PAR) to compare improvements from either increasing the screening threshold or enhancing predictive accuracy. Comprehensive simulation studies on synthetic data are provided along with capacity gap analyses, demonstrating how small adjustments in screening access can yield comparable benefits to those from complex predictive improvements.",\n  "Strengths": [\n    "Novel formulation of a policy value function that links predictive performance with screening access.",\n    "Introduction of the Prediction-Access Ratio (PAR) provides actionable insights for policy decision making.",\n    "Theoretical derivations are rigorous and are supported by extensive simulation experiments.",\n    "Clear exposition of capacity gap analysis attributing benefits from small screening adjustments."\n  ],\n  "Weaknesses": [\n    "Heavy reliance on the bivariate normal assumption which may not hold in real-world settings.",\n    "Experiments are based solely on synthetic data; the absence of evaluations on real administrative data limits the practical impact.",\n    "The residual scaling approach, while computationally efficient, remains an approximate method with unclear performance in other contexts.",\n    "Limited discussion on potential confounders or robustness to deviations from assumed error distributions."\n  ],\n  "Originality": 3,\n  "Quality": 3,\n  "Clarity": 3,\n  "Significance": 3,\n  "Questions": "1. How sensitive is the proposed framework to deviations from the bivariate normal assumption? \\n2. Can the authors provide any preliminary results or insights on applying their method to real-world datasets? \\n3. What are the limitations of the residual scaling mechanism if model errors are heavy-tailed or heteroscedastic?",\n  "Limitations": "The main limitations include the reliance on synthetic data and the simplifying assumption of the bivariate normal distribution for errors, which may not capture the complexities of real-world data. There is also limited discussion on the potential impact of model mis-specification and robustness issues. It would be beneficial to address how these limitations may affect policy outcomes in practice.",\n  "Ethical Concerns": false,\n  "Soundness": 3,\n  "Presentation": 3,\n  "Contribution": 3,\n  "Overall": 7,\n  "Confidence": 4,\n  "Decision": "Accept"\n}', True)
